\subsection*{Comment 2.3 --- Text vs.\ Eq.~(42): incremental process or convergent smoothing?}

\begin{reviewercomment}{Reviewer comment 2.3}
Section IV.B contains a logical paradox: the text describes a monotonic incremental
process, while Equation~(42) implements a convergent smoothing process. This
divergence between the mathematical definition and physical intuition renders the
mechanism logically inconsistent in maintaining high-trust stability.
\end{reviewercomment}

\rsp We thank the reviewer for this precise observation, and we agree. The
mathematics in Eq.~(42) is correct and is the definition we intend to keep; the
inconsistency was purely in the surrounding prose, which described the update as a
monotonic ratchet (``driving $\mathcal{T}_i$ upward'' / ``lowers $\mathcal{T}_i$'').
We have therefore \emph{kept Eq.~(42) unchanged and rewritten the text} so that the
physical description matches the mathematics.

\medskip
\noindent\emph{Why Eq.~(42) is a convergent smoother, not a monotonic increment.}\quad
The trust update is
\[
  \mathcal{T}_i^{(k+1)} = (1-\alpha_T)\,\mathcal{T}_i^{(k)}
        + \alpha_T\,\sigma\!\bigl(\tau_T \tilde{R}_i^{(k)}\bigr),
        \qquad \alpha_T\in(0,1).
\]
This is first-order exponential smoothing: at each episode $\mathcal{T}_i^{(k)}$ is
relaxed toward the \emph{bounded, moving} target
$\mathcal{T}_i^{\star}=\sigma(\tau_T\tilde{R}_i^{(k)})\in(0,1)$. Subtracting the
target gives
$\mathcal{T}_i^{(k+1)}-\mathcal{T}_i^{\star}
 =(1-\alpha_T)\bigl(\mathcal{T}_i^{(k)}-\mathcal{T}_i^{\star}\bigr)$,
so the map is a \emph{contraction of modulus $(1-\alpha_T)<1$}. Consequences: (i)
$\mathcal{T}_i^{(k)}$ stays in $(0,1)$ for all $k$; (ii) it converges to the
equilibrium set by current relative performance rather than growing without bound;
and (iii) sustained above-average performance holds the target above $0.5$, so trust
\emph{settles at and maintains} a high value---this is what produces stable
high trust, whereas a genuinely monotonic process would simply saturate at the
ceiling and lose all sensitivity. The paradox the reviewer identified was thus a
wording error, now removed.

\loc Section~IV-B (\emph{Trust-Modulated Tactical Exploration}), in the sentence
immediately following Eq.~(42). The monotonic-increment sentence is struck out and
replaced (deletion shown in red, addition in blue in the revised manuscript). The
new text reads:

\begin{manuscripttext}
Equation~(42) is a first-order exponential-smoothing (contraction) update, not a
monotonic accumulator: at each episode it relaxes $\mathcal{T}_i^{(k)}$ toward the
instantaneous target $\sigma(\tau_T\tilde{R}_i^{(k)})\in(0,1)$ at rate $\alpha_T$.
Because $\alpha_T\in(0,1)$ and this target is bounded in $(0,1)$, the recursion is a
contraction of modulus $(1-\alpha_T)$; hence $\mathcal{T}_i^{(k)}$ never leaves
$(0,1)$ and converges toward the moving equilibrium
$\mathcal{T}_i^{\star}=\sigma(\tau_T\tilde{R}_i^{(k)})$ set by the current relative
performance. Consistently above-average performance ($R_i^{(k)}>\mu_R$) holds this
target above $0.5$, so trust settles at and \emph{maintains} a high equilibrium---
suppressing exploration---rather than growing without bound, while persistently
below-average performance pulls the equilibrium below $0.5$ and amplifies
exploration. This convergent-smoothing behavior, not a monotonic increment, is
precisely what sustains the stable high-trust regime.
\end{manuscripttext}

\evi To make the corrected description concrete we simulate Eq.~(42) directly
(Fig.~R1). Panel~(a) shows that under sustained above-average return the update
converges to and \emph{holds} a stable high-trust equilibrium below the ceiling
($\mathcal{T}\!\approx\!0.83$), whereas the monotonic-increment reading the text
formerly implied ratchets up and pins at $1.0$. Panel~(b) shows that under
time-varying performance the trust smoothly tracks the moving target
$\sigma(\tau_T\tilde{R}_i^{(k)})$ both \emph{upward and downward}---behavior a
monotonic process cannot produce. Panel~(c) shows that from any initialization
$\mathcal{T}_i^{(0)}$ the trajectory converges to the same equilibrium, with the
deviation decaying geometrically as $(1-\alpha_T)^{k}$, confirming the contraction.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{trust_convergent_smoothing.pdf}
\captionsetup{font=small}
\caption*{\small\textbf{Fig.~R1.} The trust update of Eq.~(42) is a convergent
smoother (contraction toward a bounded, moving equilibrium), not a monotonic
increment. (a) Sustained above-average return: Eq.~(42) converges to and maintains a
stable high-trust value ($\mathcal{T}^\star=\sigma(\tau_T\tilde{R})$, dashed),
whereas a monotonic-increment reading ratchets to and pins at the ceiling. (b)
Time-varying return: trust smoothly tracks the moving target both up and down. (c)
Contraction: trajectories from any initial $\mathcal{T}_i^{(0)}$ converge to a unique
equilibrium, and the deviation decays geometrically along the $(1-\alpha_T)^k$
envelope (dotted). This is exactly the mechanism by which the update maintains stable
high trust.}
\end{figure}

